## Ingredients

**Dry**

- 3 cups (270g) old-fashioned rolled oats
- 1 cup (100g) walnut pieces
- 1/2 cup (55g) pecan pieces
- 1/4 cup (40g) flax seeds
- 2 Tbs (20g) chia seeds
- 1 1/2 tsp (4g) ground cinnamon
- 1/4 tsp kosher salt

**Wet**

- 1/2 cup (170g) honey
- 3 Tbs (40g) avocado oil
- 1 tsp (4g) vanilla extract

**Optional add-in**

- 1/2 cup (30g) unsweetened coconut flakes (added halfway through baking)

## Instructions

1. **Heat the oven.** Preheat the oven to 300°F (150°C). Line a rimmed baking sheet with parchment paper or even better use a silicon baking mat.
2. **Mix the dry ingredients.** In a large bowl, stir together the oats, walnuts, pecans, flax, chia, cinnamon, and salt.
3. **Mix the wet ingredients.** In a small bowl (or you can add in the wet directly to the dry to save effort), mix together the honey, oil, and vanilla. If the honey is thick, warm it for a few seconds first so it mixes in smoothly.
4. **Combine.** Pour the wet mixture over the dry ingredients and stir until everything is evenly coated. Let it sit for 5 minutes so the chia and flax start to soak up the liquid and get tacky. This helps the clusters hold together.
5. **Press it flat.** Spread the granola on the baking sheet and press it down firmly with a spatula into an even layer about 1/2 inch thick. Use a large half-sheet pan (13 x 18 inches), or split it between two smaller pans.
6. **Bake (for about 1/2 the total time)** Bake for 20 minutes without stirring.
7. **Mix well** and put back in oven for anouther 5 to 15 minutes or until a light brown.
7. **Mix & break apart** Take out the sheet. Use the spatula to break the granola into large pieces and flip them. If you're using coconut, scatter it over the top now.
8. **Cool without touching.** Set the sheet on a wire rack and let the granola cool completely, about 30 minutes. It will still feel soft when it comes out of the oven and crisps up as it cools. Don't stir it while it cools, so you keep the clusters.
9. **Break and store.** Break into clusters and store in an airtight container.

## Notes

- **Nutrition:** Estimated per 1/3 cup serving (about 16 servings). Net carbs are about 19g per serving. A 1/2 cup of plain 2% Greek yogurt underneath adds about 12g protein and 4g carbs.
- **Why it's more diabetes-friendly than store-bought:** Most store-bought granolas are mostly oats, with only 2–3g fiber per 1/3 cup. Many also use brown sugar, cane syrup, or dried fruit. This one mixes in walnuts, pecans, flax, and chia, so a 1/3 cup has about 23g carbs, 4g fiber, and 9g sugar, all from honey. That's on the low end of store-bought for sugar, with more fiber and healthy fat to slow down how fast the carbs are absorbed. It's still a sweetened food, so portion size matters.
- **Coconut (optional):** Unsweetened coconut flakes add toasty flavor and a little extra fat and fiber. Add them halfway so they don't burn. They add about 12 calories and 1g fat per serving. Melted coconut oil also works in place of the avocado oil.
- **Getting big clusters:** The honey is the glue, with help from the chia and flax. Pressing the granola flat and leaving it alone while it bakes and cools gives you the biggest clusters.
- **Less sugar:** Cutting the honey to 1/4 cup brings it down to about 5g sugar per serving. The granola will be less sweet and the clusters will be smaller. Replacing half the honey with allulose syrup keeps it just as sweet, and allulose browns and crisps a lot like honey.
- **Portion size:** Granola is dense in calories and some carbs, so I recomend using it with yogurt in small quanties.
- **Swaps:** Hazelnuts or more pecans can replace the walnuts. To keep it nut-free, use raw pumpkin and sunflower seeds in place of the nuts.
- **Storage:** Keeps in an airtight container at room temperature for up to 2 weeks, or in the freezer for up to 3 months. If it softens, crisp it on a baking sheet at 300°F for 5 minutes.
